The attached image (Fig. 10) illustrates that Nano-11, when combined with ovalbumin (OVA), induces a localized inflammatory response and is taken up by immune cells at the injection site in skeletal muscle. Confocal microscopy images, taken 24 hours post-injection, show recruited Mac-2+ monocytes/macrophages and MHCII+ cells (stained green) containing intracellular AF647-labeled Nano-11 (stained red) when Nano-11+OVA was administered. In contrast, injection of soluble OVA alone resulted in minimal inflammatory cell infiltration. This demonstrates Nano-11's ability to recruit antigen-presenting cells and deliver its adsorbed antigen, supporting its role as an effective vaccine adjuvant.